# configs/vpn-routes-custom.txt.example
#
# PURPOSE: User-defined CIDRs forced through VPN (awg0), overriding the
# auto-downloaded RU list. Use this when a subnet is in the RU list but
# you want it to exit via VPN anyway (e.g., RU CDN nodes that serve
# foreign content, or specific ranges you want geo-shifted).
#
# PRIORITY: Highest priority override. Applied in Stage 5c after all ISP
# routes (Stage 5 + 5b) are set. Any existing ISP route for these CIDRs
# is deleted before the VPN route is added. If a CIDR appears in both this
# file and isp-routes-custom.txt, VPN wins.
#
# FORMAT: One CIDR per line. Lines starting with # are comments, and an inline
# comment after a CIDR (e.g. "23.55.0.0/16 # Akamai") is also accepted —
# routing.sh normalize_route_line() strips it before calling ip route. The web
# admin writes this inline form, so both styles coexist in the same file.
#
# HOW TO USE:
#   1. Run: ssh pi4 "sudo /etc/splitgate/vpn-status.sh --via=isp" to identify
#      IPs exiting via ISP that you want to force through VPN
#   2. Resolve to a CIDR (via whois or ipinfo.io)
#   3. cp src/configs/vpn-routes-custom.txt.example src/configs/vpn-routes-custom.txt
#   4. Add CIDRs, one per line. Comments may be on their own line or inline
#      after the CIDR (e.g. "23.55.0.0/16 # Akamai") — both are accepted.
#   5. Run: ./src/deploy.sh — deploys to /etc/splitgate/vpn-routes-custom.txt
#      and routing.sh Stage 5c applies the VPN-force routes.
#
# VALIDATION: Malformed lines silently dropped by ip route add (2>/dev/null || true).
#
# EXAMPLE ENTRIES (remove leading # to activate):
# 77.88.0.0/18
# 5.255.255.0/24
